# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
aquinas-will-necessary.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: aquinas-will-necessary.logic.r1 has "saved → wills"; aquinas-will-necessary.logic.r1.2 and aquinas-will-necessary.logic.r1.3 have nothing from it; they differ when only "this one is saved" is true, which the second allows
aquinas-will-necessary.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered yes, which keeps aquinas-will-necessary.logic.r1.2 and aquinas-will-necessary.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "this one is saved" is true and "God wills that this one be saved" is false. Is S2, "For Augustine says (Enchiridion 103): "No one is saved, except whom God has willed to be saved.", true then?
aquinas-will-necessary.md:1: argue/negation: aquinas-will-necessary.logic.r1 P3 (S2): the sentence says "no", but P3 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "if this one is saved, then God wills that this one be saved"
aquinas-will-necessary.md:1: argue/unfaithful: aquinas-will-necessary.logic.r1 P4 (S3): the sentence has no word of "this one is saved": is it the sentence P4 came from?
